Untestable CLI: process.argv and process.env are read deep inside the logic. Save /workspace/wordfreq.mjs:

import fs from "node:fs";

export function run() {
  const file = process.argv[2];
  const topN = parseInt(process.env.TOP_N || "10", 10);
  const text = fs.readFileSync(file, "utf8").toLowerCase();
  const counts = new Map();
  for (const w of text.split(/[^a-z']+/).filter(Boolean)) {
    counts.set(w, (counts.get(w) || 0) + 1);
  }
  const top = Array.from(counts.entries()).sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1] || a[0].localeCompare(b[0])).slice(0, topN);
  for (const [w, c] of top) console.log(`${c}\t${w}`);
}
run();

Refactor into a pure core and a thin shell: wordFrequencies(text) and formatTop(counts, topN) exported from a core module with no fs/process/console anywhere in it, plus a cli.mjs that parses argv/env, reads the file, and writes to a provided stream (default process.stdout). The bare run() call at import time must go. Write wordfreq.test.mjs (node:test) against the core: tie-breaking is count desc then alphabetical, apostrophes kept ("don't"), case folding, topN larger than vocabulary, empty text. Then one integration test that spawns node cli.mjs sample.txt with TOP_N=3 via child_process.execFileSync on a fixture file it writes to a temp dir, asserting exact stdout. Everything green via node --test.

